+#ifdef PORT_PC
+/* P-793: this is a `GrJoint` table -- three `s16` -- declared as `int[2]` and
+ * cast at the use site.  Big-endian, the eight bytes `00 00 00 0A 00 00 00 00`
+ * read back as `{ .x = 0, .y = 10, .z = 0 }`: joint 0, map id 10.  Map 10 is
+ * the Home-Run ground (`grHr_StageCallbacks` entry 10, `grHomeRun_8021D680`),
+ * and joint 0 owns floor line 3, the numbered distance strip.
+ *
+ * Little-endian the same bytes read as `{ .x = 10, .y = 0, .z = 0 }`, so
+ * `Ground_801C2FE0` never matches map 10 and never calls `mpLib_80055E9C(0)`.
+ * That call is what copies each collision vertex's `pos` into `x10`/`x14`, the
+ * previous-frame position.  Every frame `grHomeRun_8021D680` re-runs
+ * `mpLib_80056758(3, 0, 0, 20000, 0)`, which stretches line 3 from its stored
+ * base out to x = 34652; with `x10`/`x14` frozen at the base vertices
+ * (28.7466 -> 14652.3018), `mpGetSpeed` remaps the fighter from the base
+ * segment onto the stretched one *every* frame -- a fixed 2.3676x scaling
+ * about x = 28.75 -- and `Fighter_procUpdate` adds that delta straight to
+ * `cur_pos`.  The sandbag's distance therefore runs away exponentially the
+ * instant it lands on the numbered ground, with zero ground or self velocity
+ * to show for it.  (Same consequence as P-772 on Zebes, different cause.)
+ *
+ * Spell the table as the type it is used at.  The console path is untouched,
+ * so the emitted `.data` and the 100% match are unchanged. */
+static GrJoint grHr_804D4998[] = { { 0, 0xA, 0 } };
+#else
 static int grHr_804D4998[2] = { 0xA, 0 };
+#endif
